<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 5: Visualising Multidimensional Data                     -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Visualising Multidimensional Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-visualising-multidimensional">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> was about trusting your data.
      This chapter is about actually <em>seeing</em> it.
      A clean, correctly coded dataset with more than two or three variables is
      still, on its own, just a table <mdash/> and a table of more than a few
      hundred rows tells a human reader almost nothing.
      The moment a dataset has real structure to it (groups, locations, time,
      several measurements per case) the question stops being
      <q>is this value right?</q> and becomes
      <q>how do I look at several things at once?</q>
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Following the Giants                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-following-the-giants">
    <title>Following the Giants</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        What does twenty years of elephant movement look like as data?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gathuku-elephant-gps">
        <caption>
          GPS relocations from 110 collared elephants collected from 2001 to
          2021 in the Laikipia-Samburu ecosystem, Kenya, overlaid on land use
          boundaries and major water courses.
          Each dot represents a single GPS fix; the density and spread of points
          reveals the elephants' collective use of the landscape over two
          decades.
          Reproduced unchanged from Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025),
          <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212,
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>, under CC
          BY-NC-ND 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/gathuku2025-fig1b.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A map of northern Kenya showing thousands of orange GPS location
              points from 110 collared elephants overlaid on a background
              showing land use boundaries in different colours and rivers in
              blue.
              The density and distribution of points reveals the collective home
              range of the tracked population across a landscape spanning
              national parks, conservancies, private ranches, community
              conservancies, and agricultural settlements.
              The concentration of points is highest in the central part of the
              study area, thinning toward the edges.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Each orange dot in the map above is one GPS location recorded by a
        collar fitted to one of 110 African savanna elephants tracked across the
        Laikipia-Samburu ecosystem in northern Kenya.
        There are 3,850,840 of them in total, collected over twenty years
        between 2001 and 2021.
        Before any statistical analysis has been done, this map shows where the
        elephants went, collectively, across a landscape that includes national
        parks, private ranches, community conservancies, and agricultural
        settlements.
        The concentration of dots, the gaps, the corridors along rivers: all of
        it is visible at a glance, in a way that no table of GPS coordinates
        could show.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the dataset at the heart of Gathuku and Wittemyer's (2025) study
        of what drives elephant home range size and movement in northern Kenya —
        and it is a good place to start thinking about why visualisation matters
        when data has more than one or two variables ("dimensions").
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-elephants-dimensions">
      <title>More than just location</title>

      <p>
        Nobody scrolls through 3,850,840 rows looking for a pattern.
        At that scale, a spreadsheet stops being a way to look at data and
        becomes just a way to store it.
        But the GPS coordinates alone are only one variable.
        Each location was also linked to information about the individual
        elephant, such as their sex and identity.
        Location was also linked to environmental measurements around that
        location; for example:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              how much vegetation was growing there (measured as NDVI, a
              satellite-derived index of greenness)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how much rainfall the area received and how variable that rainfall
              was
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how much surface water and permanent water was available nearby
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how rugged the terrain was
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how much human activity had modified the landscape (e.g.,
              settlements, agriculture, and infrastructure).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Researching what drives how much space an elephant uses, and whether it
        returns to the same areas over time, requires examining all of these
        variables (or "dimensions") together, not one at a time.
        A table of 3,850,840 rows and a dozen columns does not answer that
        question, but visualisation can be a good start.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-elephants-findings">
      <title>What the data showed</title>

      <p>
        When Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025) examined home range size alongside
        landscape variables, water emerged as the dominant factor.
        Elephants ranged over larger areas where water was scarce or
        unpredictable, and stayed within smaller areas where surface and
        permanent water were abundant.
        This makes ecological sense: when water is reliable, there is no need to
        travel far; when it is not, animals must move to find it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rainfall variability drove the same pattern: more unpredictable rainfall
        meant larger annual ranges, as elephants moved further to track
        resources that were distributed unevenly across the landscape.
      </p>

      <p>
        Human modification of the landscape had a more complex effect.
        Elephants in areas with greater human activity tended to shift their
        ranges more from one period to the next, even while staying within a
        constrained overall area — moving around within a smaller space rather
        than ranging freely.
        This is consistent with elephants responding to the risks that human
        presence creates, adjusting their movements to avoid areas of danger
        even when that means forgoing better resources.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sex differences were also clear.
        Females tended to show stronger fidelity to their ranges over time —
        returning to the same areas year after year more consistently than
        males.
        This likely reflects the more stable social structure of female-led
        family groups, which rely on accumulated knowledge of reliable water and
        food sources within a familiar area.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two of the paper's figures make these patterns visible in ways that
        summary statistics alone cannot.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gathuku-violin-annual">
        <caption>
          Annual home range estimates for female (red) and male (blue) elephants
          in the Laikipia-Samburu ecosystem, shown for two estimation methods
          and two range definitions.
          The wide, right-skewed distributions reveal enormous variability
          between individuals.
          Reproduced unchanged from Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025),
          <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212,
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>, under CC
          BY-NC-ND 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/gathuku2025-fig8.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Four violin plots arranged in a two-by-two grid.
              The left column shows 50% isopleth home range estimates and the
              right shows 95% isopleth estimates.
              Within each panel, female estimates are shown in red and male in
              blue, side by side, for both AKDE and MCP estimation methods.
              The distributions are wide and right-skewed for both sexes,
              indicating high individual variability.
              Female and male distributions overlap substantially.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The first such figure, <xref ref="fig-gathuku-violin-annual"/>, shows
        home range sizes for female and male elephants.
        You will notice it has two panels: these compare two different
        mathematical methods for estimating home range size (50% and 95%
        Isopleth), and two different definitions of what counts as the home
        range.
        The details of those methods are not important here.
        What matters is the visual figure.
        It is showing three variables at once: home range size on the vertical
        axis, estimation approach in the columns, and sex in the colour.
        Each shape, called a violin plot, shows not just an average but the full
        spread of individual estimates.
        You can see at a glance that some elephants range over vast areas while
        others stay remarkably localised.
        Furthermore, the plot makes visible the way that pattern holds across
        both sexes and both methods.
        Compare this with a table of means and standard deviations.
        Such a table would give you the averages; it would not show you the
        shape of the distribution or the degree of overlap between sexes.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gathuku-violin-overlap">
        <caption>
          Home range fidelity (overlap index) across annual and seasonal scales
          (left panel) and by sex at both scales (right panel).
          Higher values indicate greater overlap between successive home ranges
          — that is, stronger site fidelity.
          Reproduced unchanged from Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025),
          <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212,
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>, under CC
          BY-NC-ND 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/gathuku2025-fig4.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two violin plots side by side.
              The left panel shows UDOI values for annual and seasonal scales,
              with annual overlap (tan/orange violin) tending to be higher than
              seasonal overlap (blue violin), indicating stronger fidelity at
              the annual scale.
              The right panel shows UDOI values split by sex at both annual and
              seasonal scales, with four violins: female annual (red), male
              annual (blue), female seasonal (red), and male seasonal (blue).
              Females tend to show slightly higher overlap values than males,
              particularly at the annual scale, consistent with the more stable
              ranging behaviour of female-led family groups.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The second figure adds a different dimension: consistency over time.
        Rather than asking where an elephant went, it asks how consistent that
        was: "how much did the same elephant's range in one period overlap with
        its range in the next?"" An index close to 1 means the elephant used
        almost exactly the same area; close to 0 means it shifted substantially.
        Again, the technical details of how this index is calculated are not the
        point.
        The point is that the figure encodes three things at once: the scale of
        comparison (annual vs seasonal) on the horizontal axis, the degree of
        overlap on the vertical axis, and sex in the colour.
        This makes it immediately visible that elephants are generally faithful
        to their ranges year to year, and that females tend to be slightly more
        faithful than males, at both annual and seasonal scales.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-elephants-lesson">
        <title>Not more data. The same data, seen jointly.</title>

        <p>
          The data finds that water drives range size, human activity constrains
          movement, and females showing stronger fidelity than males.
          However, none of these findings are visible from any single column of
          the dataset, or even from GPS locations on their own.
          Each pattern only becomes visible once location is examined
          <em>jointly</em> with landscape variables, sex, season, and time,
          together, in the same visualisation.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <table xml:id="table-elephant-dimensions">
        <title>
          What had to be held in view at once to find these patterns
        </title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Held in view</cell>
            <cell>Variable(s)</cell>
            <cell>What it contributed</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Position</cell>
            <cell>Latitude, longitude</cell>
            <cell>Where each elephant actually was</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Water</cell>
            <cell>Surface water, permanent water, rainfall variability</cell>
            <cell>The dominant driver of range size</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Human pressure</cell>
            <cell>Human modification index</cell>
            <cell>Why elephants shift more but range less freely</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Individual</cell>
            <cell>Sex</cell>
            <cell>Where the sharpest differences in fidelity appeared</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Time</cell>
            <cell>Year, season</cell>
            <cell>How consistent ranging behaviour was over time</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Five things, held in view together, none of them new data collection —
        the same idea as every dimension added in <xref ref="sec-gapminder"/>
        later.
      </p>

      <p>
        Throughout this chapter, you will learn how to build exactly that kind
        of view: visualisations that hold several variables in view at once,
        rather than one table or one chart per variable.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Gathuku, N.M.
        and Wittemyer, G.
        (2025).
        <q>Identification of key landscape drivers of range size and 
      range shifts in African savanna elephants in northern Kenya.</q>
        <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212.
        <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>.
        Licensed under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: Gapminder                                             -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-gapminder">
    <title>Building Up Dimensions: The Gapminder Activity</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Hans Rosling's Gapminder data make the same point as the elephants, with
        a dataset you can actually hold in your hands.
        The data are completely open, and a cleaned excerpt is bundled directly
        into R as the <c>gapminder</c> package: 1,704 observations covering 142
        countries from 1952 to 2007, with one row per country per year and
        columns for <c>country</c>, <c>continent</c>, <c>year</c>,
        <c>lifeExp</c> (life expectancy), <c>pop</c> (population), and
        <c>gdpPercap</c> (GDP per person).
      </p>

      <p>
        A single row tells you almost nothing interesting.
        What made Rosling's presentation of this data famous was building it up,
        one variable at a time, into a single animated chart <mdash/> the exact
        idea behind <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants"/>, with a dataset small
        enough to work through by hand.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-2d">
      <title>Two dimensions: does money buy a longer life?</title>

      <p>
        Start with the most basic possible question: is a country's income
        related to how long its people live?
        Two variables, two axes.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(gapminder)
        library(ggplot2)

        gapminder_2007 &lt;- subset(gapminder, year == 2007)

        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
      </program>
      <p>
        Even this bare scatterplot shows a clear upward trend, though income is
        logged on the x-axis because a handful of very rich countries would
        otherwise crush the rest of the points into one corner.
        But every point is currently the same colour and the same size <mdash/>
        a Norwegian point and a Nigerian point are visually identical except for
        position.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-3d">
      <title>Three dimensions: adding continent</title>

      <p>
        Map <c>continent</c> onto colour, and a third variable appears in the
        same chart without adding a single new axis.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp, colour = continent)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
      </program>
      <p>
        The trend from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d"/> is still there, but now it
        has structure: African countries cluster toward the lower left, European
        countries toward the upper right, and the spread within each continent
        becomes visible for the first time.
        Nothing about the underlying data changed.
        One aesthetic mapping did.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-4d">
      <title>Four dimensions: adding population</title>

      <p>
        Map <c>pop</c> onto point size, and the chart gains a fourth variable,
        turning a scatterplot into a bubble chart.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                                   colour = continent, size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 15)) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
      </program>
      <p>
        China and India, similar in income and life expectancy to several
        smaller countries, now visibly dominate the chart by sheer size.
        That is real information <mdash/> population <mdash/> that a plain
        scatterplot could not have shown without a second, separate chart.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-5d">
      <title>Five dimensions: adding time</title>

      <p>
        The chart so far is a single snapshot, 2007 only.
        Rosling's original animated the chart across every year in the data,
        letting countries move as their income and life expectancy changed.
        Without animation software, the same fifth dimension <mdash/> time
        <mdash/> can be shown as small multiples, one panel per year.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                              colour = continent, size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          facet_wrap(~ year) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
      </program>
      <p>
        Read left to right, top to bottom, and entire countries visibly migrate
        across the panel: life expectancy climbing steadily almost everywhere,
        even where income barely moves; the occasional sharp reversal, a bubble
        dropping backward in a single panel, worth investigating rather than
        dismissing as noise.
        (If you have the <c>gganimate</c> package installed, the same five
        variables can be drawn as a single moving chart,
        <c>transition_time(year)</c>, closer to Rosling's original.)
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-summary">
      <title>What just happened</title>

      <p>
        Five variables, five aesthetics, and not one extra table.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-gapminder-dimensions">
        <title>Dimensions added to the Gapminder chart, one at a time</title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Dimension</cell>
            <cell>Aesthetic</cell>
            <cell>Variable</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1st &amp; 2nd</cell>
            <cell>x, y position</cell>
            <cell><c>gdpPercap</c>, <c>lifeExp</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3rd</cell>
            <cell>colour</cell>
            <cell><c>continent</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>4th</cell>
            <cell>size</cell>
            <cell><c>pop</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>5th</cell>
            <cell>facet / animation frame</cell>
            <cell><c>year</c></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Each new variable was added as a new <term>aesthetic mapping</term>, not
        a new chart, a new axis, or a new table.
        That is the whole technique this chapter is teaching, and
        <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants"/> was the same idea before it had a
        name: GPS location, land cover, protection status, sex and season, held
        together in one analysis rather than one table per variable, is what
        turned 834,138 rows of coordinates into a finding about forest
        preference, sex differences and a corridor gone quiet.
        Five variables again, in substance if not in a single ggplot call
        <mdash/> the same move as <xref ref="ss-gapminder-4d"/>, just with a
        resource-selection model standing in for <c>colour =</c> and
        <c>size =</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bryan, J.
        <c>gapminder</c> R package, data curated by the Gapminder Foundation (
        <url href="https://www.gapminder.org" visual="gapminder.org"/> ), based
        on the work of Hans Rosling.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: a short gallery                                       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-viz-gallery">
    <title>A Short Gallery: When Two Dimensions Weren't Enough</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The elephants and Gapminder are recent examples of a much older idea.
        Long before <c>ggplot2</c>, before spreadsheets, before computers,
        people were solving exactly this problem <mdash/> a table or a simple
        chart that cannot show what actually matters <mdash/> by finding a way
        to hold several variables in one image at once.
        Four examples, spanning more than 150 years, make the case better than
        any single modern one can on its own.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-minard">
      <title>Minard's map of Napoleon's Russian campaign (1869)</title>

      <p>
        Charles Joseph Minard was a retired French civil engineer, 80 years old,
        when he published a single lithograph that Edward Tufte later called
        <q>possibly the best statistical graphic ever drawn.</q> It depicts
        Napoleon's catastrophic 1812 invasion of Russia and the retreat that
        followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        A bar chart of monthly troop losses would need several separate charts
        to show army size, geographic position, direction of travel, and the
        temperature that killed so many soldiers on the retreat.
        Minard fit all of it into one image.
        A tan band, its width scaled at precisely one millimetre per ten
        thousand men, traces the army's advance eastward toward Moscow, drawn
        directly onto a map so that position and army size are shown together.
        A black band, same scale, traces the retreat back.
        Beneath the map, a separate strip plots temperature against date along
        the retreat route, so the collapse in troop numbers can be read directly
        against the cold that caused so much of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Six variables, one image: geography, direction of travel, troop numbers,
        the route itself, temperature, and time.
        The tan band starts near the Polish border at 422,000 men and narrows
        steadily marching into Russia, losing soldiers to disease and combat
        even before Moscow.
        The retreat band is where the graphic becomes devastating: it thins
        almost to nothing by the time it reaches the same border, months later,
        at around 10,000 survivors, while the temperature strip beneath falls
        below <m>-30^\circ\text{C}</m> at the worst of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        A table of monthly casualty figures existed and always existed.
        It could not make the collapse visible the way the combined graphic
        does, because a table cannot show a retreating army's position, its
        shrinking size, and the falling temperature all bearing on each other at
        once, in a single glance.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Minard, C.J.
        (1869).
        <em>Carte figurative des
        pertes successives en hommes de l'Armée Française dans la campagne
        de Russie 1812<ndash/>1813.</em>
        Described in Tufte, E.R.
        (1983).
        <em>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information.</em> Cheshire, CT:
        Graphics Press.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-nightingale">
      <title>Nightingale's Coxcomb (1858)</title>

      <p>
        In 1858, Florence Nightingale published a report built almost entirely
        around one number she needed British officials to stop ignoring: of the
        soldiers who died during the Crimean War, the overwhelming majority died
        not in battle but of preventable disease in military hospitals.
      </p>

      <p>
        She could have reported a single national mortality rate.
        Instead she built what became known as the <term>coxcomb</term>, or rose
        diagram: a circular chart of twelve wedges, one per month, each wedge's
        area scaled to that month's deaths, each wedge divided by colour into
        three causes <mdash/> preventable disease, wounds from battle, and all
        other causes.
        As with Minard's map, the point is not one summary number but a pattern
        developing over time, visible only because time, cause, and magnitude
        are encoded in the same image.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pattern is stark.
        In the war's first winter, before a Sanitary Commission arrived in March
        1855 to overhaul hygiene in the camps and hospitals, the wedges for
        disease dwarf the wedges for wounds, month after month.
        After the Commission's reforms, the wedges shrink sharply.
        A table of the same monthly totals contains identical information; it
        does not make the argument nearly as forcefully, because a table asks
        the reader to do the comparing themselves, while the coxcomb does the
        comparing for them, at a glance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nightingale was a trained statistician petitioning a government that had
        shown little appetite for reading tables of army mortality statistics.
        The diagram is credited as a direct influence on the sanitary reforms,
        in the military and more broadly, that followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Nightingale, F.
        (1858).
        <em>Notes on Matters
        Affecting the Health, Efficiency, and Hospital Administration of
        the British Army, Founded Chiefly on the Experience of the Late
        War.</em>
        London: Harrison and Sons.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-snow">
      <title>John Snow's cholera map (1854)</title>

      <p>
        In late August 1854, cholera broke out in Soho, London, killing over 600
        people within a few weeks, concentrated overwhelmingly around Broad
        Street.
        Cholera was widely believed at the time to spread through <q>miasma,</q>
        bad air, rather than water; there was no accepted mechanism connecting
        the disease to anything as specific as a single pump.
      </p>

      <p>
        The physician John Snow suspected otherwise, and tested it by mapping
        rather than tabulating.
        On a street plan of the neighbourhood, he marked a dash for every
        recorded death at the address where it occurred, and marked every local
        water pump.
        The map made a pattern visible that a table of addresses and death
        counts could not: deaths clustered tightly around a single pump on Broad
        Street and thinned with distance from it, while households with their
        own water supply, or a nearby brewery whose workers drank almost none of
        the local water, had almost no cases at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Location and death count, combined in a single image, did what neither
        could do alone.
        Snow presented the map to the local parish and persuaded them to remove
        the handle from the Broad Street pump.
        The outbreak was already declining by that point, so the act did not
        single-handedly end it <mdash/> that specific detail is often overstated
        <mdash/> but the map itself provided the first strong evidence that
        cholera spread through contaminated water rather than air, and a later
        well inspection found the pump's water supply sat near a leaking
        cesspit, connecting the map's pattern to an actual physical cause.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Snow, J.
        (1855).
        <em>On the Mode of
        Communication of Cholera</em> (2nd ed.).
        London: John Churchill.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-challenger">
      <title>The Challenger disaster (1986)</title>

      <p>
        On 28 January 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds
        after launch, killing all seven crew members.
        The immediate cause was the failure of an O-ring seal on a solid rocket
        booster, a component engineers at Morton Thiokol, the manufacturer, had
        already flagged as a concern in cold weather.
      </p>

      <p>
        The night before launch, those engineers argued against flying, worried
        specifically about the forecast temperature.
        Ambient temperature at launch was later measured at 36°F, with the
        temperature at the failed joint itself estimated at 28 <ndash/> 31°F
        <mdash/> roughly 15 degrees colder than any previous launch.
        Their argument did not persuade NASA management, and the launch went
        ahead.
      </p>

      <p>
        Edward Tufte's later analysis, in <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997),
        argues that part of why the engineers failed to persuade anyone was how
        their evidence was presented.
        Under time pressure, their chart plotted O-ring damage only for flights
        that had already shown some, leaving out every fully successful flight.
        Displayed that way, the relationship between cold and damage does not
        obviously stand out.
        Tufte reconstructed the same data as a single chart of temperature
        against O-ring damage for <em>every</em> flight, successful or not.
        Plotted that way, the relationship is difficult to miss, and the
        forecast launch temperature sits clearly outside the range of every
        previous flight.
      </p>

      <p>
        This case is genuinely contested, and worth reading that way rather than
        as a clean morality tale.
        Some historians of the disaster argue Tufte's account overstates how
        much a better chart alone would have changed the outcome, given the
        institutional and schedule pressures NASA management was under
        regardless of what any chart showed, and some note that even Tufte's
        reconstructed chart is not fully faithful to the data available that
        night.
        What is not contested is the underlying point: a chart showing only part
        of the relevant data <mdash/> here, only the flights with problems
        <mdash/> can hide exactly the pattern a reader most needs to see.
        Showing all of it is what makes a relationship visible or invisible.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Presidential Commission on the Space Shuttle
        Challenger Accident (1986).
        <em>Report to the President.</em> Washington, DC: US Government Printing
        Office.
        Tufte, E.R.
        (1997).
        <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and
        Narrative.</em>
        Cheshire, CT: Graphics Press.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  Chapter - STACK question
Chapter - Illustrative example in R (Gapminder bit?)
Exercises
  <objectives xml:id="objectives-visualising-multidimensional">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain why a large, clean dataset can still be unreadable without
          visualisation.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Add variables to a plot using aesthetic mappings (colour, size, facet)
          rather than separate tables or charts.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Build up a multidimensional visualisation incrementally, one variable
          at a time, and describe what each addition reveals.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Choose an appropriate encoding (colour vs.
          size vs.
          facet vs.
          animation) for a given variable and dataset.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-visualising-multidimensional">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-elephants-variables">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants"/> pairs each elephant's GPS
          location with several other variables: vegetation cover, distance to
          water, human infrastructure, sex, season, and time of day.
          <xref ref="ss-mara-findings"/> describes findings that came from
          examining location jointly with land cover, protection status, sex and
          season.
          Pick one variable not explicitly named in that combination <mdash/>
          time of day, or distance to water <mdash/> and describe, in a sentence
          or two, what mapping it onto colour, size, or facet might reveal that
          the described analysis would not.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-gapminder-1952">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Adapt the code in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-4d"/> to show 1952 instead
          of 2007.
          Compare the two charts.
          Which continents have moved the most in the fifty-five years between
          them, and along which axis?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-flamingo-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> from
          <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>.
          Build a single plot that encodes at least four variables at once (for
          example: bill length and body mass on the axes, species on colour,
          lake on facet).
          What does the combined view show that a single-variable summary of
          each column, taken separately, would not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-too-many-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-gapminder-5d"/> uses position, colour, size, and facet
          together, four aesthetics beyond the two axes.
          Is there a point at which adding another variable to the same chart
          would make it harder to read rather than easier?
          Argue for a specific upper limit, and say what you would do with a
          fifth or sixth variable instead of cramming it into the same plot.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>